  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nBetween Wednesday and Wednesday night the weakened 500 MB \ntrough axis will finally cross Iowa, bringing somewhat thicker \nmid/high level clouds. However, the trough will be largely \nwashed out by that time and a deep near-surface dry layer will \npersist, once again reducing any rain chances. Thereafter, a \nhigh-amplitude ridging pattern will build across the central \nU.S., with the axis stretching all the way from Mexico up to \nHudson Bay by Thursday evening. This will maintain dry and \nnotably warmer weather at the end of the work week. Both \nThursday and Friday high temperatures will likely range in the\nmid to upper 70s, and a few areas could even touch 80 degrees.\n\nBy Friday, even as the 500 MB ridge axis moves over Iowa, a \ndeep trough will build over the western U.S. and subsequently \nslide northeastward into the Upper Midwest, shunting the \npreceding ridge southeastward and bringing a return of rain \nchances followed eventually by a surface trough/cool front \nthrough Iowa. While this general scenario is likely to occur, \nlong-range models still vary considerably in the details of the\nevolution, timing and magnitude of any rain chances, strength \nof the surface trough, etc. As such, persistent low (20-30%) \nrain chances are forecast next weekend but it is likely these \nwill eventually narrow to a shorter period of higher POPs as \nconfidence in timing increases later this week.",
